{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07260","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07260","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07260","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting","familyName":"Dang","name":"Dang, Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vassilis","familyName":"Kostakos","name":"Kostakos, Vassilis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael J.","familyName":"Witbrock","name":"Witbrock, Michael J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HealthSLM-Bench: Benchmarking Small Language Models for Mobile and Wearable Healthcare Monitoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T22:36:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:11:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T12:03:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:28:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T11:48:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:56:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-30T12:04:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-01T00:58:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-29T07:27:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:28:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mobile and wearable healthcare monitoring play a vital role in facilitating timely interventions, managing chronic health conditions, and ultimately improving individuals' quality of life. Previous studies on large language models (LLMs) have highlighted their impressive generalization abilities and effectiveness in healthcare prediction tasks. However, most LLM-based healthcare solutions are cloud-based, which raises significant privacy concerns and results in increased memory usage and latency. To address these challenges, there is growing interest in compact models, Small Language Models (SLMs), which are lightweight and designed to run locally and efficiently on mobile and wearable devices. Nevertheless, how well these models perform in healthcare prediction remains largely unexplored. We systematically evaluated SLMs on health prediction tasks using zero-shot, few-shot, and instruction fine-tuning approaches, and deployed the best performing fine-tuned SLMs on mobile devices to evaluate their real-world efficiency and predictive performance in practical healthcare scenarios. Our results show that SLMs can achieve performance comparable to LLMs while offering substantial gains in efficiency and privacy. However, challenges remain, particularly in handling class imbalance and few-shot scenarios. These findings highlight SLMs, though imperfect in their current form, as a promising solution for next-generation, privacy-preserving healthcare monitoring."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 6 tables, 6 figures. Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on GenAI4Health"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07260","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:37:07Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:37:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:19:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07706","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07706","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07706","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yildiray","familyName":"Kabak","name":"Kabak, Yildiray","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gokce B. Laleci","familyName":"Erturkmen","name":"Erturkmen, Gokce B. Laleci","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mert","familyName":"Gencturk","name":"Gencturk, Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuncay","familyName":"Namli","name":"Namli, Tuncay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. Anil","familyName":"Sinaci","name":"Sinaci, A. Anil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruben Alcantud","familyName":"Corcoles","name":"Corcoles, Ruben Alcantud","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cristina Gomez","familyName":"Ballesteros","name":"Ballesteros, Cristina Gomez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Abizanda","name":"Abizanda, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Asuman","familyName":"Dogac","name":"Dogac, Asuman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FHIR-RAG-MEDS: Integrating HL7 FHIR with Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models for Enhanced Medical Decision Support"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T13:10:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:43:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-26T07:39:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-03T00:29:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3390/ai7070246","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this study, we propose FHIR-RAG-MEDS system that aims to integrate Health Level 7 Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (HL7 FHIR) with a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)-based system to improve personalized medical decision support on evidence-based clinical guidelines, emphasizing the need for research in practical applications. In the evolving landscape of medical decision support systems, integrating advanced technologies such as RAG and HL7 FHIR can significantly enhance clinical decision-making processes. Despite the potential of these technologies, there is limited research on their integration in practical applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, submitted to Journal AI specifically to the special issue \"LLMs and AI Agents in Biomedical and Health Sciences\", under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07706","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:47:40Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:47:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-03T02:12:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinxuan","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Xinxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongna","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tongna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suchao","familyName":"An","name":"An, Suchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ercong","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Ercong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guodong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Query Expansion in the Age of Pre-trained and Large Language Models: A Comprehensive Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T14:31:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:48:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-25T13:13:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:36:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-07T13:52:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-08T01:02:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern information retrieval must reconcile short, ambiguous queries with increasingly diverse and dynamic corpora. Query expansion (QE) remains a core technique for mitigating vocabulary mismatch, but its design space has been reshaped by pre-trained and large language models (PLMs/LLMs). This survey reviews QE methods in the PLM/LLM era and provides a unified view of the emerging landscape. We first summarize how different model families enable new expansion behaviors, including stronger contextualization, more controllable generation, and instruction-following. We then organize recent techniques along four complementary design dimensions: where expansion is injected in the pipeline, how it is grounded and interacts with corpus evidence, how it is learned or aligned, and how structured knowledge such as knowledge graphs is incorporated. Beyond taxonomy, we synthesize application patterns and deployment considerations across representative retrieval settings, highlighting practical trade-offs among effectiveness, controllability, grounding quality, and operating cost. Finally, we outline open challenges and future directions toward more reliable, safe, efficient, and continually adaptive QE under real-world constraints."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages,10 figures,6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:49:50Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:49:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-08T12:51:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grant","familyName":"Pinkert","name":"Pinkert, Grant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanli","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DuoServe-MoE: Dual-Phase Expert Prefetch and Caching for LLM Inference QoS Assurance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T04:00:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:20:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T05:45:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:29:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as Internet/Web services (LLM-as-a-Service) with strict latency Service-Level Objectives (SLOs) under tight GPU memory budgets. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models improve quality and throughput via sparse expert activation, but serving them efficiently is challenging because expert weights dominate memory footprint and incur costly host--device transfers when offloaded. Moreover, MoE serving exhibits a phase disparity: the prefill phase tends to activate experts densely across many tokens, while the decode phase activates only a few experts per step. A uniform expert loading/caching policy across phases leads to either peak-memory blowup (prefill) or tail-latency inflation (decode). We present DuoServe-MoE, a QoS-oriented MoE serving system that decouples prefill and decode and applies phase-specialized expert scheduling. For prefill, DuoServe-MoE uses a two-stream CUDA pipeline to overlap expert prefetching with non-MoE computation, reducing expert residency time and peak GPU memory. For decode, it employs a lightweight layer-level predictor trained offline from activation traces to prefetch only likely experts without model changes. Experiments on representative MoE LLMs show that DuoServe-MoE improves TTFT by up to $5.34\\times$ and end-to-end latency by up to $7.55\\times$ over representative baselines, while maintaining low runtime GPU memory usage under resource-constrained deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:39:54Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:39:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:23:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Heyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangmou","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Xiangmou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengzhou","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Pengzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongru","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zongru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhaoxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuosheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VeriOS: Query-Driven Proactive Human-Agent-GUI Interaction for Trustworthy OS Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T09:46:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:34:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T03:25:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:19:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-03T02:36:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:17:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid progress of multimodal large language models, operating system (OS) agents become increasingly capable of automating tasks through on-device graphical user interfaces (GUIs). However, most existing OS agents are designed for idealized settings, whereas real-world environments often present untrustworthy conditions. To mitigate risks of over-execution in such scenarios, we propose a query-driven human-agent-GUI interaction framework that enables OS agents to decide when to query humans for more reliable task completion. Built upon this framework, we introduce VeriOS-Agent, a trustworthy OS agent trained with a three-stage learning paradigm that falicitate the decoupling and utilization of meta-knowledge by supervised fine-tuning and group relative policy optimization. Concretely, VeriOS-Agent autonomously executes actions in normal conditions while proactively querying humans in untrustworthy scenarios. Experiments show that VeriOS-Agent improves the average step-wise success rate by 19.72\\% in over the strongest baselines, without compromising normal performance. VeriOS-Agent significantly improves performance in untrustworthy scenarios while maintaining comparable performance in trustworthy scenarios. Analysis highlights VeriOS-Agent's rationality, generalizability, and scalability. The codes, datasets and models are available at https://github.com/Wuzheng02/VeriOS."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:43:58Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:43:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-06T01:48:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07968","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07968","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07968","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Haas","name":"Haas, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gal","familyName":"Yona","name":"Yona, Gal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"D'Antonio","name":"D'Antonio, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasha","familyName":"Goldshtein","name":"Goldshtein, Sasha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dipanjan","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Dipanjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SimpleQA Verified: A Reliable Factuality Benchmark to Measure Parametric Knowledge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:53:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:56:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T12:28:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:55:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce SimpleQA Verified, a 1,000-prompt benchmark for evaluating Large Language Model (LLM) short-form factuality based on OpenAI's SimpleQA. It addresses critical limitations in OpenAI's benchmark, including noisy and incorrect labels, topical biases, and question redundancy. SimpleQA Verified was created through a rigorous multi-stage filtering process involving de-duplication, topic balancing, and source reconciliation to produce a more reliable and challenging evaluation set, alongside improvements in the autorater prompt. On this new benchmark, Gemini 2.5 Pro achieves a state-of-the-art F1-score of 55.6, outperforming other frontier models, including GPT-5. This work provides the research community with a higher-fidelity tool to track genuine progress in parametric model factuality and to mitigate hallucinations. The benchmark dataset, evaluation code, and leaderboard are available at: https://www.kaggle.com/benchmarks/deepmind/simpleqa-verified."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07968","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:54Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijian","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaran","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Jiaran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason Klein","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jason Klein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanting","familyName":"Miao","name":"Miao, Yanting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shirui","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Shirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Choice of Divergence: A Neglected Key to Mitigating Diversity Collapse in Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Reward"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T06:34:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:24:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T09:26:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:35:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T08:10:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-12T01:32:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-03T07:36:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:32:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A central paradox in fine-tuning Large Language Models (LLMs) with Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Reward (RLVR) is the frequent degradation of multi-attempt performance (Pass@k) despite improvements in single-attempt accuracy (Pass@1). This is often accompanied by catastrophic forgetting, where models lose previously acquired skills. While various methods have been proposed, the choice and function of the divergence term have been surprisingly unexamined as a proactive solution. We argue that standard RLVR objectives -- both those using the mode-seeking reverse KL-divergence and those forgoing a divergence term entirely -- lack a crucial mechanism for knowledge retention. The reverse-KL actively accelerates this decay by narrowing the policy, while its absence provides no safeguard against the model drifting from its diverse knowledge base. We propose a fundamental shift in perspective: using the divergence term itself as the solution. Our framework, Diversity-Preserving Hybrid RL (DPH-RL), leverages mass-covering f-divergences (like forward-KL and JS-divergence) to function as a rehearsal mechanism. By continuously referencing the initial policy, this approach forces the model to maintain broad solution coverage. Extensive experiments on math and SQL generation demonstrate that DPH-RL not only resolves the Pass@k degradation but improves both Pass@1 and Pass@k in- and out-of-domain. Additionally, DPH-RL is more training-efficient because it computes f-divergence using generator functions, requiring only sampling from the initial policy and no online reference model. Our work highlights a crucial, overlooked axis for improving RLVR, demonstrating that the proper selection of a divergence measure is a powerful tool for building more general and diverse reasoning models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:41:07Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:41:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:17:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07403","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07403","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07403","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weichu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Weichu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghuan","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Minghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningning","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Ningning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaofan","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Chaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingpeng","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Lingpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ngai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LongEmotion: Measuring Emotional Intelligence of Large Language Models in Long-Context Interaction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T05:32:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:22:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-11T12:20:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:45:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have made significant progress in Emotional Intelligence (EI) and long-context modeling. However, existing benchmarks often overlook the fact that emotional information processing unfolds as a continuous long-context process. To address the absence of multidimensional EI evaluation in long-context inference and explore model performance under more challenging conditions, we present LongEmotion, a benchmark that encompasses a diverse suite of tasks targeting the assessment of models' capabilities in Emotion Recognition, Knowledge Application, and Empathetic Generation, with an average context length of 15,341 tokens. To enhance performance under realistic constraints, we introduce the Collaborative Emotional Modeling (CoEM) framework, which integrates Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) and multi-agent collaboration to improve models' EI in long-context scenarios. We conduct a detailed analysis of various models in long-context settings, investigating how reasoning mode activation, RAG-based retrieval strategies, and context-length adaptability influence their EI performance. Our project page is: https://longemotion.github.io/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical Report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07403","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:40:28Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:40:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:25:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07457","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07457","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07457","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Sungkyunkwan University"],"givenName":"Shakhzod","familyName":"Yuldoshkhujaev","name":"Yuldoshkhujaev, Shakhzod","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Sungkyunkwan University"],"givenName":"Mijin","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Mijin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["University of Tennessee"],"givenName":"Doowon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Doowon","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Stony Brook University"],"givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Nikiforakis","name":"Nikiforakis, Nick","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Sungkyunkwan University"],"givenName":"Hyungjoon","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Hyungjoon","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Decade-long Landscape of Advanced Persistent Threats: Longitudinal Analysis and Global Trends"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T07:26:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:26:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T04:19:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:19:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3719027.3765085","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An advanced persistent threat (APT) refers to a covert, long-term cyberattack, typically conducted by state-sponsored actors, targeting critical sectors and often remaining undetected for long periods. In response, collective intelligence from around the globe collaborates to identify and trace surreptitious activities, generating substantial documentation on APT campaigns publicly available on the web. While prior works predominantly focus on specific aspects of APT cases, such as detection, evaluation, cyber threat intelligence, and dataset creation, limited attention has been devoted to revisiting and investigating these scattered dossiers in a longitudinal manner. The objective of our study is to fill the gap by offering a macro perspective, connecting key insights and global trends in past APT attacks. We systematically analyze six reliable sources-three focused on technical reports and another three on threat actors-examining 1,509 APT dossiers (24,215 pages) spanning 2014-2023, and identifying 603 unique APT groups worldwide. To efficiently unearth relevant information, we employ a hybrid methodology that combines rule-based information retrieval with large-language-model-based search techniques. Our longitudinal analysis reveals shifts in threat actor activities, global attack vectors, changes in targeted sectors, and relationships between cyberattacks and significant events such as elections or wars, which provide insights into historical patterns in APT evolution. Over the past decade, 154 countries have been affected, primarily using malicious documents and spear phishing as dominant initial infiltration vectors, with a noticeable decline in zero-day exploitation since 2016. Furthermore, we present our findings through interactive visualization tools, such as an APT map or flow diagram, to facilitate intuitive understanding of global patterns and trends in APT activities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 13 figures (including subfigures), 11 tables. In the Proceedings of the ACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security (CCS) 2025. Received Distinguished Paper Award"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07457","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:41:44Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:41:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anjiang","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Anjiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianran","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Tianran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yogesh","familyName":"Seenichamy","name":"Seenichamy, Yogesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anne","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Anne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Azalia","familyName":"Mirhoseini","name":"Mirhoseini, Azalia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Aiken","name":"Aiken, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Astra: A Multi-Agent System for GPU Kernel Performance Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T08:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:31:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-02T21:26:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:08:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GPU kernel optimization has long been a central challenge at the intersection of high-performance computing and machine learning. Efficient kernels are crucial for accelerating large language model (LLM) training and serving, yet attaining high performance typically requires extensive manual tuning. Compiler-based systems reduce some of this burden, but still demand substantial manual design and engineering effort. Recently, researchers have explored using LLMs for GPU kernel generation, though prior work has largely focused on translating high-level PyTorch modules into CUDA code. In this work, we introduce Astra, the first LLM-based multi-agent system for GPU kernel optimization. Unlike previous approaches, Astra starts from existing CUDA implementations extracted from SGLang, a widely deployed framework for serving LLMs, rather than treating PyTorch modules as the specification. Within Astra, specialized LLM agents collaborate through iterative code generation, testing, profiling, and planning to produce kernels that are both correct and high-performance. On kernels from SGLang, Astra achieves an average speedup of 1.32x using zero-shot prompting with OpenAI o4-mini. A detailed case study further demonstrates that LLMs can autonomously apply loop transformations, optimize memory access patterns, exploit CUDA intrinsics, and leverage fast math operations to yield substantial performance gains. Our work highlights multi-agent LLM systems as a promising new paradigm for GPU kernel optimization. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/Anjiang-Wei/Astra."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:42:53Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:42:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-04T18:40:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07424","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07424","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07424","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunseung","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Hyunseung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dasom","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Dasom","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"DaEun","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, DaEun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sooyohn","familyName":"Nam","name":"Nam, Sooyohn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hwajung","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Hwajung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Feed-O-Meter: Investigating AI-Generated Mentee Personas as Interactive Agents for Scaffolding Design Feedback Practice"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T06:31:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:23:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-02T13:14:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:45:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective feedback, including critique and evaluation, helps designers develop design concepts and refine their ideas, supporting informed decision-making throughout the iterative design process. However, in studio-based design courses, students often struggle to provide feedback due to a lack of confidence and fear of being judged, which limits their ability to develop essential feedback-giving skills. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) suggest that role-playing with AI agents can let learners engage in multi-turn feedback without the anxiety of external judgment or the time constraints of real-world settings. Yet prior studies have raised concerns that LLMs struggle to behave like real people in role-play scenarios, diminishing the educational benefits of these interactions. Therefore, designing AI-based agents that effectively support learners in practicing and developing intellectual reasoning skills requires more than merely assigning the target persona's personality and role to the agent. By addressing these issues, we present Feed-O-Meter, a novel system that employs carefully designed LLM-based agents to create an environment in which students can practice giving design feedback. The system enables users to role-play as mentors, providing feedback to an AI mentee and allowing them to reflect on how that feedback impacts the AI mentee's idea development process. A user study (N=24) indicated that Feed-O-Meter increased participants' engagement and motivation through role-switching and helped them adjust feedback to be more comprehensible for an AI mentee. Based on these findings, we discuss future directions for designing systems to foster feedback skills in design education."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07424","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:40:58Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:40:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-03T02:50:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07939","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07939","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07939","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katsuaki","familyName":"Nakano","name":"Nakano, Katsuaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reza","familyName":"Fayyazi","name":"Fayyazi, Reza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shanchieh Jay","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Shanchieh Jay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Zuzak","name":"Zuzak, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guided Reasoning in LLM-Driven Penetration Testing Using Structured Attack Trees"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:19:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:55:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T18:20:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T02:03:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) have driven interest in automating cybersecurity penetration testing workflows, offering the promise of faster and more consistent vulnerability assessment for enterprise systems. Existing LLM agents for penetration testing primarily rely on self-guided reasoning, which can produce inaccurate or hallucinated procedural steps. As a result, the LLM agent may undertake unproductive actions, such as exploiting unused software libraries or generating cyclical responses that repeat prior tactics. In this work, we propose a guided reasoning pipeline for penetration testing LLM agents that incorporates a deterministic task tree built from the MITRE ATT\u0026amp;CK Matrix, a proven penetration testing kll chain, to constrain the LLM's reaoning process to explicitly defined tactics, techniques, and procedures. This anchors reasoning in proven penetration testing methodologies and filters out ineffective actions by guiding the agent towards more productive attack procedures. To evaluate our approach, we built an automated penetration testing LLM agent using three LLMs (Llama-3-8B, Gemini-1.5, and GPT-4) and applied it to navigate 10 HackTheBox cybersecurity exercises with 103 discrete subtasks representing real-world cyberattack scenarios. Our proposed reasoning pipeline guided the LLM agent through 71.8\\%, 72.8\\%, and 78.6\\% of subtasks using Llama-3-8B, Gemini-1.5, and GPT-4, respectively. Comparatively, the state-of-the-art LLM penetration testing tool using self-guided reasoning completed only 13.5\\%, 16.5\\%, and 75.7\\% of subtasks and required 86.2\\%, 118.7\\%, and 205.9\\% more model queries. This suggests that incorporating a deterministic task tree into LLM reasoning pipelines can enhance the accuracy and efficiency of automated cybersecurity assessments"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07939","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:13Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:17:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sven","familyName":"Kirchner","name":"Kirchner, Sven","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nils","familyName":"Purschke","name":"Purschke, Nils","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ross","familyName":"Greer","name":"Greer, Ross","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alois C.","familyName":"Knoll","name":"Knoll, Alois C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DepthVision: Enabling Robust Vision-Language Models with GAN-Based LiDAR-to-RGB Synthesis for Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T07:42:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:27:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T13:49:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:50:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring reliable autonomous operation when visual input is degraded remains a key challenge in intelligent vehicles and robotics. We present DepthVision, a multimodal framework that enables Vision--Language Models (VLMs) to exploit LiDAR data without any architectural changes or retraining. DepthVision synthesizes dense, RGB-like images from sparse LiDAR point clouds using a conditional GAN with an integrated refiner, and feeds these into off-the-shelf VLMs through their standard visual interface. A Luminance-Aware Modality Adaptation (LAMA) module fuses synthesized and real camera images by dynamically weighting each modality based on ambient lighting, compensating for degradation such as darkness or motion blur. This design turns LiDAR into a drop-in visual surrogate when RGB becomes unreliable, effectively extending the operational envelope of existing VLMs. We evaluate DepthVision on real and simulated datasets across multiple VLMs and safety-critical tasks, including vehicle-in-the-loop experiments. The results show substantial improvements in low-light scene understanding over RGB-only baselines while preserving full compatibility with frozen VLM architectures. These findings demonstrate that LiDAR-guided RGB synthesis is a practical pathway for integrating range sensing into modern vision-language systems for autonomous driving."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:41:52Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:41:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:16:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07325","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07325","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07325","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alyssa","familyName":"Unell","name":"Unell, Alyssa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noel C. F.","familyName":"Codella","name":"Codella, Noel C. F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Preston","name":"Preston, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peniel","familyName":"Argaw","name":"Argaw, Peniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen-wai","familyName":"Yim","name":"Yim, Wen-wai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelalem","familyName":"Gero","name":"Gero, Zelalem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cliff","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Cliff","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajesh","familyName":"Jena","name":"Jena, Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Horvitz","name":"Horvitz, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amanda K.","familyName":"Hall","name":"Hall, Amanda K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruican Rachel","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Ruican Rachel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiachen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrey","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Shrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mu","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Mu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Lungren","name":"Lungren, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoifung","familyName":"Poon","name":"Poon, Hoifung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CancerGUIDE: Cancer Guideline Understanding via Internal Disagreement Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T01:49:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:15:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T18:38:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:58:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides evidence-based guidelines for cancer treatment. Translating complex patient presentations into guideline-compliant treatment recommendations is time-intensive, requires specialized expertise, and is prone to error. Advances in large language model (LLM) capabilities promise to reduce the time required to generate treatment recommendations and improve accuracy. We present an LLM agent-based approach to automatically generate guideline-concordant treatment trajectories for patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Our contributions are threefold. First, we construct a novel longitudinal dataset of 121 cases of NSCLC patients that includes clinical encounters, diagnostic results, and medical histories, each expertly annotated with the corresponding NCCN guideline trajectories by board-certified oncologists. Second, we demonstrate that existing LLMs possess domain-specific knowledge that enables high-quality proxy benchmark generation for both model development and evaluation, achieving strong correlation (Spearman coefficient r=0.88, RMSE = 0.08) with expert-annotated benchmarks. Third, we develop a hybrid approach combining expensive human annotations with model consistency information to create both the agent framework that predicts the relevant guidelines for a patient, as well as a meta-classifier that verifies prediction accuracy with calibrated confidence scores for treatment recommendations (AUROC=0.800), a critical capability for communicating the accuracy of outputs, custom-tailoring tradeoffs in performance, and supporting regulatory compliance. This work establishes a framework for clinically viable LLM-based guideline adherence systems that balance accuracy, interpretability, and regulatory requirements while reducing annotation costs, providing a scalable pathway toward automated clinical decision support."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07325","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:38:38Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:38:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-07T03:05:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07759","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07759","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07759","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Minghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miyang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Miyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianrui","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Tianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yishuai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yishuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Siqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ercong","familyName":"Nie","name":"Nie, Ercong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guodong","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Survey of Long-Document Retrieval in the PLM and LLM Era"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T13:57:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:46:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-25T13:44:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The proliferation of long-form documents presents a fundamental challenge to information retrieval (IR), as their length, dispersed evidence, and complex structures demand specialized methods beyond standard passage-level techniques. This survey provides the first comprehensive treatment of long-document retrieval (LDR), consolidating methods, challenges, and applications across three major eras. We systematize the evolution from classical lexical and early neural models to modern pre-trained (PLM) and large language models (LLMs), covering key paradigms like passage aggregation, hierarchical encoding, efficient attention, and the latest LLM-driven re-ranking and retrieval techniques. Beyond the models, we review domain-specific applications, specialized evaluation resources, and outline critical open challenges such as efficiency trade-offs, multimodal alignment, and faithfulness. This survey aims to provide both a consolidated reference and a forward-looking agenda for advancing long-document retrieval in the era of foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07759","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:48:59Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:49:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:01:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07142","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07142","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07142","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zhiyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"D'Souza","name":"D'Souza, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Purpose-oriented Topic Model Evaluation enabled by Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Digital Libraries (cs.DL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T18:46:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-24T00:39:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s00799-025-00429-5","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study presents a framework for automated evaluation of dynamically evolving topic models using Large Language Models (LLMs). Topic modeling is essential for organizing and retrieving scholarly content in digital library systems, helping users navigate complex and evolving knowledge domains. However, widely used automated metrics, such as coherence and diversity, often capture only narrow statistical patterns and fail to explain semantic failures in practice. We introduce a purpose-oriented evaluation framework that employs nine LLM-based metrics spanning four key dimensions of topic quality: lexical validity, intra-topic semantic soundness, inter-topic structural soundness, and document-topic alignment soundness. The framework is validated through adversarial and sampling-based protocols, and is applied across datasets spanning news articles, scholarly publications, and social media posts, as well as multiple topic modeling methods and open-source LLMs. Our analysis shows that LLM-based metrics provide interpretable, robust, and task-relevant assessments, uncovering critical weaknesses in topic models such as redundancy and semantic drift, which are often missed by traditional metrics. These results support the development of scalable, fine-grained evaluation tools for maintaining topic relevance in dynamic datasets. All code and data supporting this work are accessible at https://github.com/zhiyintan/topic-model-LLMjudgment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in International Journal on Digital Libraries (IJDL)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07142","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:34:21Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:34:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-24T02:14:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07755","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07755","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07755","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rochana Prih","familyName":"Hastuti","name":"Hastuti, Rochana Prih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rian Adam","familyName":"Rajagede","name":"Rajagede, Rian Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mansour Al","familyName":"Ghanim","name":"Ghanim, Mansour Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengxin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Mengxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Factuality Beyond Coherence: Evaluating LLM Watermarking Methods for Medical Texts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T13:54:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:45:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-20T03:20:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:15:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are adapted to sensitive domains such as medicine, their fluency raises safety risks, particularly regarding provenance and accountability. Watermarking embeds detectable patterns to mitigate these risks, yet its reliability in medical contexts remains untested. Existing benchmarks focus on detection-quality tradeoffs and overlook factual risks. In medical text, watermarking often reweights low-entropy tokens, which are highly predictable and often carry critical medical terminology. Shifting these tokens can cause inaccuracy and hallucinations, risks that prior general-domain benchmarks fail to capture.\n We propose a medical-focused evaluation workflow that jointly assesses factual accuracy and coherence. Using GPT-Judger and further human validation, we introduce the Factuality-Weighted Score (FWS), a composite metric prioritizing factual accuracy beyond coherence to guide watermarking deployment in medical domains. Our evaluation shows current watermarking methods substantially compromise medical factuality, with entropy shifts degrading medical entity representation. These findings underscore the need for domain-aware watermarking approaches that preserve the integrity of medical content."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025 Findings. Camera Ready"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07755","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:48:53Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:48:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:47:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiyu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunfang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Chunfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuefeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xuefeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Xi","name":"Xi, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaoyao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yaoyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinglan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qinglan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlan","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Junlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Generalized Routing: Model and Agent Orchestration for Adaptive and Efficient Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T10:15:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:35:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T02:09:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:11:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) and domain-specific AI agents has greatly expanded the ecosystem of AI-powered services. User queries, however, are highly diverse and often span multiple domains and task types, resulting in a complex and heterogeneous landscape. This diversity presents a fundamental routing challenge: how to accurately direct each query to an appropriate execution unit while optimizing both performance and efficiency. To address this, we propose MoMA (Mixture of Models and Agents), a generalized routing framework that integrates both LLM and agent-based routing. Built upon a deep understanding of model and agent capabilities, MoMA effectively handles diverse queries through precise intent recognition and adaptive routing strategies, achieving an optimal balance between efficiency and cost. Specifically, we construct a detailed training dataset to profile the capabilities of various LLMs under different routing model structures, identifying the most suitable tasks for each LLM. During inference, queries are dynamically routed to the LLM with the best cost-performance efficiency. We also introduce an efficient agent selection strategy based on a context-aware state machine and dynamic masking. Experimental results demonstrate that the MoMA router offers superior cost-efficiency and scalability compared to existing approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:44:23Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:44:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:49:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixuan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Abbasi","name":"Abbasi, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PersonaFuse: A Personality Activation-Driven Framework for Enhancing Human-LLM Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T03:39:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:19:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-11T09:42:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-12T00:26:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate remarkable capabilities across various fields. These developments have led to more direct communication between humans and LLMs in various situations, such as social companionship and psychological support. However, LLMs often exhibit limitations in emotional perception and social competence during real-world conversations. These limitations partly originate from their inability to adapt their communication style and emotional expression to different social and task contexts. In this work, we introduce PersonaFuse, a novel LLM post-training framework that enables LLMs to adapt and express different personalities for varying situations. Inspired by Trait Activation Theory and the Big Five personality model, PersonaFuse employs a Mixture-of-Expert architecture that combines persona adapters with a dynamic routing network, enabling contextual trait expression. Experimental results show that PersonaFuse substantially outperforms baseline models across multiple dimensions of social-emotional intelligence. Importantly, these gains are achieved without sacrificing general reasoning ability or model safety, which remain common limitations of direct prompting and supervised fine-tuning approaches. PersonaFuse also delivers consistent improvements in downstream human-centered applications, such as mental health counseling and review-based customer service. Finally, human preference evaluations against leading LLMs, including GPT-4o and DeepSeek, demonstrate that PersonaFuse achieves competitive response quality despite its comparatively smaller model size. These findings demonstrate that PersonaFuse offers a theoretically grounded and practical approach for developing social-emotional enhanced LLMs, marking a significant advancement toward more human-centric AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:39:41Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:39:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-12T01:49:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07941","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07941","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07941","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangcai","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Liangcai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiong","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Chenxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ImportSnare: Directed \"Code Manual\" Hijacking in Retrieval-Augmented Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:21:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:55:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code generation has emerged as a pivotal capability of Large Language Models(LLMs), revolutionizing development efficiency for programmers of all skill levels. However, the complexity of data structures and algorithmic logic often results in functional deficiencies and security vulnerabilities in generated code, reducing it to a prototype requiring extensive manual debugging. While Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) can enhance correctness and security by leveraging external code manuals, it simultaneously introduces new attack surfaces.\n In this paper, we pioneer the exploration of attack surfaces in Retrieval-Augmented Code Generation (RACG), focusing on malicious dependency hijacking. We demonstrate how poisoned documentation containing hidden malicious dependencies (e.g., matplotlib_safe) can subvert RACG, exploiting dual trust chains: LLM reliance on RAG and developers' blind trust in LLM suggestions. To construct poisoned documents, we propose ImportSnare, a novel attack framework employing two synergistic strategies: 1)Position-aware beam search optimizes hidden ranking sequences to elevate poisoned documents in retrieval results, and 2)Multilingual inductive suggestions generate jailbreaking sequences to manipulate LLMs into recommending malicious dependencies. Through extensive experiments across Python, Rust, and JavaScript, ImportSnare achieves significant attack success rates (over 50% for popular libraries such as matplotlib and seaborn) in general, and is also able to succeed even when the poisoning ratio is as low as 0.01%, targeting both custom and real-world malicious packages. Our findings reveal critical supply chain risks in LLM-powered development, highlighting inadequate security alignment for code generation tasks. To support future research, we will release the multilingual benchmark suite and datasets. The project homepage is https://importsnare.github.io."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted by the ACM Conference on Computer and Communications Security (CCS) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07941","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:16Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:53:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07933","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07933","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07933","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanni Vidulige Ishan","familyName":"Perera","name":"Perera, Wanni Vidulige Ishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"liang","name":"liang, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Junyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking Android with AI: A Deep Dive into LLM-Powered Exploitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:17:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:54:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid evolution of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Large Language Models (LLMs) has opened up new opportunities in the area of cybersecurity, especially in the exploitation automation landscape and penetration testing. This study explores Android penetration testing automation using LLM-based tools, especially PentestGPT, to identify and execute rooting techniques. Through a comparison of the traditional manual rooting process and exploitation methods produced using AI, this study evaluates the efficacy, reliability, and scalability of automated penetration testing in achieving high-level privilege access on Android devices. With the use of an Android emulator (Genymotion) as the testbed, we fully execute both traditional and exploit-based rooting methods, automating the process using AI-generated scripts. Secondly, we create a web application by integrating OpenAI's API to facilitate automated script generation from LLM-processed responses. The research focuses on the effectiveness of AI-enabled exploitation by comparing automated and manual penetration testing protocols, by determining LLM weaknesses and strengths along the way. We also provide security suggestions of AI-enabled exploitation, including ethical factors and potential misuse. The findings exhibit that while LLMs can significantly streamline the workflow of exploitation, they need to be controlled by humans to ensure accuracy and ethical application. This study adds to the increasing body of literature on AI-powered cybersecurity and its effect on ethical hacking, security research, and mobile device security."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07933","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:53:05Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:53:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:53:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07860","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07860","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07860","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanzhi","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Guanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Keung","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Yu-Keung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijun","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Peijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dapeng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Dapeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinqiao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yinqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linqi","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Linqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KLIPA: A Knowledge Graph and LLM-Driven QA Framework for IP Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T15:40:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:51:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effectively managing intellectual property is a significant challenge. Traditional methods for patent analysis depend on labor-intensive manual searches and rigid keyword matching. These approaches are often inefficient and struggle to reveal the complex relationships hidden within large patent datasets, hindering strategic decision-making. To overcome these limitations, we introduce KLIPA, a novel framework that leverages a knowledge graph and a large language model (LLM) to significantly advance patent analysis. Our approach integrates three key components: a structured knowledge graph to map explicit relationships between patents, a retrieval-augmented generation(RAG) system to uncover contextual connections, and an intelligent agent that dynamically determines the optimal strategy for resolving user queries. We validated KLIPA on a comprehensive, real-world patent database, where it demonstrated substantial improvements in knowledge extraction, discovery of novel connections, and overall operational efficiency. This combination of technologies enhances retrieval accuracy, reduces reliance on domain experts, and provides a scalable, automated solution for any organization managing intellectual property, including technology corporations and legal firms, allowing them to better navigate the complexities of strategic innovation and competitive intelligence."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07860","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:51:24Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:51:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:51:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07846","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07846","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07846","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amay","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Amay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Si","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Si","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Aligning LLMs for the Classroom with Knowledge-Based Retrieval -- A Comparative RAG Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T15:22:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:50:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models like ChatGPT are increasingly used in classrooms, but they often provide outdated or fabricated information that can mislead students. Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) improves reliability of LLMs by grounding responses in external resources. We investigate two accessible RAG paradigms, vector-based retrieval and graph-based retrieval to identify best practices for classroom question answering (QA). Existing comparative studies fail to account for pedagogical factors such as educational disciplines, question types, and practical deployment costs. Using a novel dataset, EduScopeQA, of 3,176 questions across academic subjects, we measure performance on various educational query types, from specific facts to broad thematic discussions. We also evaluate system alignment with a dataset of systematically altered textbooks that contradict the LLM's latent knowledge. We find that OpenAI Vector Search RAG (representing vector-based RAG) performs well as a low-cost generalist, especially for quick fact retrieval. On the other hand, GraphRAG Global excels at providing pedagogically rich answers to thematic queries, and GraphRAG Local achieves the highest accuracy with the dense, altered textbooks when corpus integrity is critical. Accounting for the 10-20x higher resource usage of GraphRAG (representing graph-based RAG), we show that a dynamic branching framework that routes queries to the optimal retrieval method boosts fidelity and efficiency. These insights provide actionable guidelines for educators and system designers to integrate RAG-augmented LLMs into learning environments effectively."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07846","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:51:04Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:51:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:51:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07764","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07764","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07764","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanpeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yanpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentSentinel: An End-to-End and Real-Time Security Defense Framework for Computer-Use Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T13:59:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:46:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have been increasingly integrated into computer-use agents, which can autonomously operate tools on a user's computer to accomplish complex tasks. However, due to the inherently unstable and unpredictable nature of LLM outputs, they may issue unintended tool commands or incorrect inputs, leading to potentially harmful operations. Unlike traditional security risks stemming from insecure user prompts, tool execution results from LLM-driven decisions introduce new and unique security challenges. These vulnerabilities span across all components of a computer-use agent. To mitigate these risks, we propose AgentSentinel, an end-to-end, real-time defense framework designed to mitigate potential security threats on a user's computer. AgentSentinel intercepts all sensitive operations within agent-related services and halts execution until a comprehensive security audit is completed. Our security auditing mechanism introduces a novel inspection process that correlates the current task context with system traces generated during task execution. To thoroughly evaluate AgentSentinel, we present BadComputerUse, a benchmark consisting of 60 diverse attack scenarios across six attack categories. The benchmark demonstrates a 87% average attack success rate on four state-of-the-art LLMs. Our evaluation shows that AgentSentinel achieves an average defense success rate of 79.6%, significantly outperforming all baseline defenses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07764","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:49:06Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:49:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:49:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07727","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07727","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07727","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songkai","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Songkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaorui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhaorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Di","name":"Di, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benben","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Benben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaodong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xiaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyi","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xiaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoE-Compression: How the Compression Error of Experts Affects the Inference Accuracy of MoE Model?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T13:28:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:44:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the widespread application of Mixture of Experts (MoE) reasoning models in the field of LLM learning, efficiently serving MoE models under limited GPU memory constraints has emerged as a significant challenge. Offloading the non-activated experts to main memory has been identified as an efficient approach to address such a problem, while it brings the challenges of transferring the expert between the GPU memory and main memory. We need to explore an efficient approach to compress the expert and analyze how the compression error affects the inference performance.\n To bridge this gap, we propose employing error-bounded lossy compression algorithms (such as SZ3 and CuSZp) to compress non-activated experts, thereby reducing data transfer overhead during MoE inference. We conduct extensive experiments across various benchmarks and present a comprehensive analysis of how compression-induced errors in different experts affect overall inference accuracy. The results indicate that experts in the shallow layers, which are primarily responsible for the attention mechanism and the transformation of input tokens into vector representations, exhibit minimal degradation in inference accuracy when subjected to bounded errors. In contrast, errors in the middle-layer experts, which are central to model reasoning, significantly impair inference accuracy. Interestingly, introducing bounded errors in the deep-layer experts, which are mainly responsible for instruction following and output integration, can sometimes lead to improvements in inference accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07727","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:48:09Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:48:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:48:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07680","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07680","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07680","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sachit","familyName":"Menon","name":"Menon, Sachit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmet","familyName":"Iscen","name":"Iscen, Ahmet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arsha","familyName":"Nagrani","name":"Nagrani, Arsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias","familyName":"Weyand","name":"Weyand, Tobias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carl","familyName":"Vondrick","name":"Vondrick, Carl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cordelia","familyName":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Cordelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CAViAR: Critic-Augmented Video Agentic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T17:59:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:41:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video understanding has seen significant progress in recent years, with models' performance on perception from short clips continuing to rise. Yet, multiple recent benchmarks, such as LVBench, Neptune, and ActivityNet-RTL, show performance wanes for tasks requiring complex reasoning on videos as queries grow more complex and videos grow longer. In this work, we ask: can existing perception capabilities be leveraged to successfully perform more complex video reasoning? In particular, we develop a large language model agent given access to video modules as subagents or tools. Rather than following a fixed procedure to solve queries as in previous work such as Visual Programming, ViperGPT, and MoReVQA, the agent uses the results of each call to a module to determine subsequent steps. Inspired by work in the textual reasoning domain, we introduce a critic to distinguish between instances of successful and unsuccessful sequences from the agent. We show that the combination of our agent and critic achieve strong performance on the previously-mentioned datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07680","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:47:02Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:47:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:47:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanchao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yanchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nan","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Nan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoLoRAG: Bootstrapping Document Understanding via Multi-modal Logic-aware Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-06T00:59:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:40:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Document Understanding is a foundational AI capability with broad applications, and Document Question Answering (DocQA) is a key evaluation task. Traditional methods convert the document into text for processing by Large Language Models (LLMs), but this process strips away critical multi-modal information like figures. While Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) address this limitation, their constrained input size makes multi-page document comprehension infeasible. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) methods mitigate this by selecting relevant pages, but they rely solely on semantic relevance, ignoring logical connections between pages and the query, which is essential for reasoning.\n To this end, we propose MoLoRAG, a logic-aware retrieval framework for multi-modal, multi-page document understanding. By constructing a page graph that captures contextual relationships between pages, a lightweight VLM performs graph traversal to retrieve relevant pages, including those with logical connections often overlooked. This approach combines semantic and logical relevance to deliver more accurate retrieval. After retrieval, the top-$K$ pages are fed into arbitrary LVLMs for question answering. To enhance flexibility, MoLoRAG offers two variants: a training-free solution for easy deployment and a fine-tuned version to improve logical relevance checking. Experiments on four DocQA datasets demonstrate average improvements of 9.68% in accuracy over LVLM direct inference and 7.44% in retrieval precision over baselines. Codes and datasets are released at https://github.com/WxxShirley/MoLoRAG."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP Main 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:46:42Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:46:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:46:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07642","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07642","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07642","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sascha","familyName":"Kaltenpoth","name":"Kaltenpoth, Sascha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Müller","name":"Müller, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Getting In Contract with Large Language Models -- An Agency Theory Perspective On Large Language Model Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T12:10:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:39:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Adopting Large language models (LLMs) in organizations potentially revolutionizes our lives and work. However, they can generate off-topic, discriminating, or harmful content. This AI alignment problem often stems from misspecifications during the LLM adoption, unnoticed by the principal due to the LLM's black-box nature. While various research disciplines investigated AI alignment, they neither address the information asymmetries between organizational adopters and black-box LLM agents nor consider organizational AI adoption processes. Therefore, we propose LLM ATLAS (LLM Agency Theory-Led Alignment Strategy) a conceptual framework grounded in agency (contract) theory, to mitigate alignment problems during organizational LLM adoption. We conduct a conceptual literature analysis using the organizational LLM adoption phases and the agency theory as concepts. Our approach results in (1) providing an extended literature analysis process specific to AI alignment methods during organizational LLM adoption and (2) providing a first LLM alignment problem-solution space."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Presented at the 19th International Conference on Wirtschaftsinformatik 2024, W\\\"urzburg, Germany https://aisel.aisnet.org/wi2024/91/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07642","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:46:03Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:46:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:46:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07595","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07595","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07595","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiva Sai Krishna Anand","familyName":"Tokal","name":"Tokal, Shiva Sai Krishna Anand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaibhav","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Vaibhav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anand","familyName":"Eswaran","name":"Eswaran, Anand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Praveen","familyName":"Jayachandran","name":"Jayachandran, Praveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yogesh","familyName":"Simmhan","name":"Simmhan, Yogesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentX: Towards Orchestrating Robust Agentic Workflow Patterns with FaaS-hosted MCP Services"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T11:07:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:37:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) has rapidly transformed various fields including code generation, text summarization, image generation and so on. Agentic AI is a recent evolution that further advances this by coupling the decision making and generative capabilities of LLMs with actions that can be performed using tools. While seemingly powerful, Agentic systems often struggle when faced with numerous tools, complex multi-step tasks,and long-context management to track history and avoid hallucinations. Workflow patterns such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) and ReAct help address this. Here, we define a novel agentic workflow pattern, AgentX, composed of stage designer, planner, and executor agents that is competitive or better than the state-of-the-art agentic patterns. We also leverage Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools, and propose two alternative approaches for deploying MCP servers as cloud Functions as a Service (FaaS). We empirically evaluate the success rate, latency and cost for AgentX and two contemporary agentic patterns, ReAct and Magentic One, using these the FaaS and local MCP server alternatives for three practical applications. This highlights the opportunities and challenges of designing and deploying agentic workflows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07595","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:44:57Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:44:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:44:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrey","familyName":"Sakhovskiy","name":"Sakhovskiy, Andrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elena","familyName":"Tutubalina","name":"Tutubalina, Elena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BALI: Enhancing Biomedical Language Representations through Knowledge Graph and Language Model Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; H.3.3; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T10:59:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:36:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3726302.3729901","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, there has been substantial progress in using pretrained Language Models (LMs) on a range of tasks aimed at improving the understanding of biomedical texts. Nonetheless, existing biomedical LLMs show limited comprehension of complex, domain-specific concept structures and the factual information encoded in biomedical Knowledge Graphs (KGs). In this work, we propose BALI (Biomedical Knowledge Graph and Language Model Alignment), a novel joint LM and KG pre-training method that augments an LM with external knowledge by the simultaneous learning of a dedicated KG encoder and aligning the representations of both the LM and the graph. For a given textual sequence, we link biomedical concept mentions to the Unified Medical Language System (UMLS) KG and utilize local KG subgraphs as cross-modal positive samples for these mentions. Our empirical findings indicate that implementing our method on several leading biomedical LMs, such as PubMedBERT and BioLinkBERT, improves their performance on a range of language understanding tasks and the quality of entity representations, even with minimal pre-training on a small alignment dataset sourced from PubMed scientific abstracts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 1 figure, published in \"The 48th International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval (SIGIR 2025)\""}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-10T01:44:48Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:44:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:44:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07555","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07555","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07555","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangrong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Xiangrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Avoiding Knowledge Edit Skipping in Multi-hop Question Answering with Guided Decomposition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T09:49:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:34:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In a rapidly evolving world where information updates swiftly, knowledge in large language models (LLMs) becomes outdated quickly. Retraining LLMs is not a cost-effective option, making knowledge editing (KE) without modifying parameters particularly necessary. We find that although existing retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)-based KE methods excel at editing simple knowledge, they struggle with KE in multi-hop question answering due to the issue of \"edit skipping\", which refers to skipping the relevant edited fact in inference. In addition to the diversity of natural language expressions of knowledge, edit skipping also arises from the mismatch between the granularity of LLMs in problem-solving and the facts in the edited memory. To address this issue, we propose a novel Iterative Retrieval-Augmented Knowledge Editing method with guided decomposition (IRAKE) through the guidance from single edited facts and entire edited cases. Experimental results demonstrate that IRAKE mitigates the failure of editing caused by edit skipping and outperforms state-of-the-art methods for KE in multi-hop question answering."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in EMNLP Findings 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07555","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:44:01Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:44:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:44:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07512","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07512","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07512","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weize","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Weize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiji","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ALLabel: Three-stage Active Learning for LLM-based Entity Recognition using Demonstration Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T08:47:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:31:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many contemporary data-driven research efforts in the natural sciences, such as chemistry and materials science, require large-scale, high-performance entity recognition from scientific datasets. Large language models (LLMs) have increasingly been adopted to solve the entity recognition task, with the same trend being observed on all-spectrum NLP tasks. The prevailing entity recognition LLMs rely on fine-tuned technology, yet the fine-tuning process often incurs significant cost. To achieve a best performance-cost trade-off, we propose ALLabel, a three-stage framework designed to select the most informative and representative samples in preparing the demonstrations for LLM modeling. The annotated examples are used to construct a ground-truth retrieval corpus for LLM in-context learning. By sequentially employing three distinct active learning strategies, ALLabel consistently outperforms all baselines under the same annotation budget across three specialized domain datasets. Experimental results also demonstrate that selectively annotating only 5\\%-10\\% of the dataset with ALLabel can achieve performance comparable to the method annotating the entire dataset. Further analyses and ablation studies verify the effectiveness and generalizability of our proposal."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07512","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:43:01Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:43:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:43:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youngjin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Youngjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wookjin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wookjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong Ki","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hong Ki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sang Hyun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Sang Hyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constraint-Compliant Network Optimization through Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T08:16:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:30:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work develops an LLM-based optimization framework ensuring strict constraint satisfaction in network optimization. While LLMs possess contextual reasoning capabilities, existing approaches often fail to enforce constraints, causing infeasible solutions. Unlike conventional methods that address average constraints, the proposed framework integrates a natural language-based input encoding strategy to restrict the solution space and guarantee feasibility. For multi-access edge computing networks, task allocation is optimized while minimizing worst-case latency. Numerical evaluations demonstrate LLMs as a promising tool for constraint-aware network optimization, offering insights into their inference capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:42:33Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:42:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:42:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07475","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07475","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07475","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saumya","familyName":"Goswami","name":"Goswami, Saumya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Kurra","name":"Kurra, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HALT-RAG: A Task-Adaptable Framework for Hallucination Detection with Calibrated NLI Ensembles and Abstention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T07:58:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:28:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting content that contradicts or is unsupported by a given source text is a critical challenge for the safe deployment of generative language models. We introduce HALT-RAG, a post-hoc verification system designed to identify hallucinations in the outputs of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines. Our flexible and task-adaptable framework uses a universal feature set derived from an ensemble of two frozen, off-the-shelf Natural Language Inference (NLI) models and lightweight lexical signals. These features are used to train a simple, calibrated, and task-adapted meta-classifier. Using a rigorous 5-fold out-of-fold (OOF) training protocol to prevent data leakage and produce unbiased estimates, we evaluate our system on the HaluEval benchmark. By pairing our universal feature set with a lightweight, task-adapted classifier and a precision-constrained decision policy, HALT-RAG achieves strong OOF F1-scores of 0.7756, 0.9786, and 0.7391 on the summarization, QA, and dialogue tasks, respectively. The system's well-calibrated probabilities enable a practical abstention mechanism, providing a reliable tool for balancing model performance with safety requirements."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07475","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:42:09Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:42:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:42:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07389","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07389","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07389","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sankalp Tattwadarshi","familyName":"Swain","name":"Swain, Sankalp Tattwadarshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anshika","familyName":"Krishnatray","name":"Krishnatray, Anshika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhruv","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Dhruv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jagat Sesh","familyName":"Challa","name":"Challa, Jagat Sesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Talking with Oompa Loompas: A novel framework for evaluating linguistic acquisition of LLM agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T05:09:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:21:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing evaluation studies on linguistic competence of large language models (LLM agents) have focused primarily on vocabulary learning, morphological rule induction, syntactic generalization, pragmatic inference, and cross-linguistic transfer. However, none assess whether LLM agents can acquire a language through pattern recognition and interactive feedback, a central feature of human language acquisition. We propose a novel experimental framework in which an LLM agent is evaluated on its ability to acquire and use a newly constructed language (Tinkatongue) in conversation with a bot that understands only Tinkatongue. Our findings show that LLM agents fail to establish a conversation within 100 responses, yet they adopt distinct strategies that mirror human approaches to language learning. The results suggest a new direction for evaluation benchmarks and open pathways to model designs that learn more effectively from interactive feedback."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07389","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:40:08Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:40:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:40:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunxi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Cunxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongjian","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Rongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chia-Tung","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Chia-Tung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxing","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Haoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Autonomous Code Evolution Meets NP-Completeness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T03:28:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:19:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently shown strong coding abilities, enabling not only static code generation but also iterative code self-evolving through agentic frameworks. Recently, AlphaEvolve \\cite{novikov2025alphaevolve} demonstrated that LLM-based coding agents can autonomously improve algorithms and surpass human experts, with scopes limited to isolated kernels spanning hundreds of lines of code. Inspired by AlphaEvolve, we present SATLUTION, the first framework to extend LLM-based code evolution to the full repository scale, encompassing hundreds of files and tens of thousands of lines of C/C++ code. Targeting Boolean Satisfiability (SAT), the canonical NP-complete problem and a cornerstone of both theory and applications. SATLUTION orchestrates LLM agents to directly evolve solver repositories under strict correctness guarantees and distributed runtime feedback, while simultaneously self-evolving its own evolution policies and rules. Starting from SAT Competition 2024 codebases and benchmark, SATLUTION evolved solvers that decisively outperformed the human-designed winners of the SAT Competition 2025, and also surpassed both 2024 and 2025 champions on the 2024 benchmarks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:39:37Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:39:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:39:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07334","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07334","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07334","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunnong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yunnong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengwei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Chengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liuqing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Liuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SpecifyUI: Supporting Iterative UI Design Intent Expression through Structured Specifications and Generative AI"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T02:19:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:16:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) promise to accelerate UI design, yet current tools struggle with two fundamentals: externalizing designers' intent and controlling iterative change. We introduce SPEC, a structured, parameterized, hierarchical intermediate representation that exposes UI elements as controllable parameters. Building on SPEC, we present SpecifyUI, an interactive system that extracts SPEC from UI references via region segmentation and vision-language models, composes UIs across multiple sources, and supports targeted edits at global, regional, and component levels. A multi-agent generator renders SPEC into high-fidelity designs, closing the loop between intent expression and controllable generation. Quantitative experiments show SPEC-based generation more faithfully captures reference intent than prompt-based baselines. In a user study with 16 professional designers, SpecifyUI significantly outperformed Stitch on intent alignment, design quality, controllability, and overall experience in human-AI co-creation. Our results position SPEC as a specification-driven paradigm that shifts LLM-assisted design from one-shot prompting to iterative, collaborative workflows."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07334","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:38:51Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:38:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:38:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07315","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07315","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07315","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongfei","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Hongfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongru","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zeming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SafeToolBench: Pioneering a Prospective Benchmark to Evaluating Tool Utilization Safety in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-09T01:31:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:15:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have exhibited great performance in autonomously calling various tools in external environments, leading to better problem solving and task automation capabilities. However, these external tools also amplify potential risks such as financial loss or privacy leakage with ambiguous or malicious user instructions. Compared to previous studies, which mainly assess the safety awareness of LLMs after obtaining the tool execution results (i.e., retrospective evaluation), this paper focuses on prospective ways to assess the safety of LLM tool utilization, aiming to avoid irreversible harm caused by directly executing tools. To this end, we propose SafeToolBench, the first benchmark to comprehensively assess tool utilization security in a prospective manner, covering malicious user instructions and diverse practical toolsets. Additionally, we propose a novel framework, SafeInstructTool, which aims to enhance LLMs' awareness of tool utilization security from three perspectives (i.e., \\textit{User Instruction, Tool Itself, and Joint Instruction-Tool}), leading to nine detailed dimensions in total. We experiment with four LLMs using different methods, revealing that existing approaches fail to capture all risks in tool utilization. In contrast, our framework significantly enhances LLMs' self-awareness, enabling a more safe and trustworthy tool utilization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07315","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:38:24Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:38:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:38:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07253","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07253","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07253","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian","familyName":"Killingback","name":"Killingback, Julian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamed","familyName":"Zamani","name":"Zamani, Hamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Information Retrieval Models on Complex Retrieval Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T22:11:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are incredible and versatile tools for text-based tasks that have enabled countless, previously unimaginable, applications. Retrieval models, in contrast, have not yet seen such capable general-purpose models emerge. To achieve this goal, retrieval models must be able to perform complex retrieval tasks, where queries contain multiple parts, constraints, or requirements in natural language. These tasks represent a natural progression from the simple, single-aspect queries that are used in the vast majority of existing, commonly used evaluation sets. Complex queries naturally arise as people expect search systems to handle more specific and often ambitious information requests, as is demonstrated by how people use LLM-based information systems. Despite the growing desire for retrieval models to expand their capabilities in complex retrieval tasks, there exist limited resources to assess the ability of retrieval models on a comprehensive set of diverse complex tasks. The few resources that do exist feature a limited scope and often lack realistic settings making it hard to know the true capabilities of retrieval models on complex real-world retrieval tasks. To address this shortcoming and spur innovation in next-generation retrieval models, we construct a diverse and realistic set of complex retrieval tasks and benchmark a representative set of state-of-the-art retrieval models. Additionally, we explore the impact of LLM-based query expansion and rewriting on retrieval quality. Our results show that even the best models struggle to produce high-quality retrieval results with the highest average nDCG@10 of only 0.346 and R@100 of only 0.587 across all tasks. Although LLM augmentation can help weaker models, the strongest model has decreased performance across all metrics with all rewriting techniques."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07253","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:36:56Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:36:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:36:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07163","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07163","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07163","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haike","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Sequential Reranking: Reranker-Guided Search Improves Reasoning Intensive Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T19:24:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:06:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widely used retrieve-and-rerank pipeline faces two critical limitations: they are constrained by the initial retrieval quality of the top-k documents, and the growing computational demands of LLM-based rerankers restrict the number of documents that can be effectively processed. We introduce Reranker-Guided-Search (RGS), a novel approach that bypasses these limitations by directly retrieving documents according to reranker preferences rather than following the traditional sequential reranking method. Our method uses a greedy search on proximity graphs generated by approximate nearest neighbor algorithms, strategically prioritizing promising documents for reranking based on document similarity. Experimental results demonstrate substantial performance improvements across multiple benchmarks: 3.5 points on BRIGHT, 2.9 on FollowIR, and 5.1 on M-BEIR, all within a constrained reranker budget of 100 documents. Our analysis suggests that, given a fixed pair of embedding and reranker models, strategically selecting documents to rerank can significantly improve retrieval accuracy under limited reranker budget."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07163","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:34:50Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:34:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:34:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07127","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07127","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07127","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonardo","familyName":"Zini","name":"Zini, Leonardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elia","familyName":"Frigieri","name":"Frigieri, Elia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastiano","familyName":"Aloscari","name":"Aloscari, Sebastiano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcello","familyName":"Generali","name":"Generali, Marcello","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Dodi","name":"Dodi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Dosen","name":"Dosen, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SVGauge: Towards Human-Aligned Evaluation for SVG Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T18:28:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:04:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generated Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) images demand evaluation criteria tuned to their symbolic and vectorial nature: criteria that existing metrics such as FID, LPIPS, or CLIPScore fail to satisfy. In this paper, we introduce SVGauge, the first human-aligned, reference based metric for text-to-SVG generation. SVGauge jointly measures (i) visual fidelity, obtained by extracting SigLIP image embeddings and refining them with PCA and whitening for domain alignment, and (ii) semantic consistency, captured by comparing BLIP-2-generated captions of the SVGs against the original prompts in the combined space of SBERT and TF-IDF. Evaluation on the proposed SHE benchmark shows that SVGauge attains the highest correlation with human judgments and reproduces system-level rankings of eight zero-shot LLM-based generators more faithfully than existing metrics. Our results highlight the necessity of vector-specific evaluation and provide a practical tool for benchmarking future text-to-SVG generation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at 23rd edition of International Conference on Image Analysis and Processing 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07127","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:33:59Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:34:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:34:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07050","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07050","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07050","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan Manuel","familyName":"Contreras","name":"Contreras, Juan Manuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Evaluation of Gender Bias Across 13 Large Multimodal Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; F.2.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T15:54:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:02:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large multimodal models (LMMs) have revolutionized text-to-image generation, but they risk perpetuating the harmful social biases in their training data. Prior work has identified gender bias in these models, but methodological limitations prevented large-scale, comparable, cross-model analysis. To address this gap, we introduce the Aymara Image Fairness Evaluation, a benchmark for assessing social bias in AI-generated images. We test 13 commercially available LMMs using 75 procedurally-generated, gender-neutral prompts to generate people in stereotypically-male, stereotypically-female, and non-stereotypical professions. We then use a validated LLM-as-a-judge system to score the 965 resulting images for gender representation. Our results reveal (p \u0026lt; .001 for all): 1) LMMs systematically not only reproduce but actually amplify occupational gender stereotypes relative to real-world labor data, generating men in 93.0% of images for male-stereotyped professions but only 22.5% for female-stereotyped professions; 2) Models exhibit a strong default-male bias, generating men in 68.3% of the time for non-stereotyped professions; and 3) The extent of bias varies dramatically across models, with overall male representation ranging from 46.7% to 73.3%. Notably, the top-performing model de-amplified gender stereotypes and approached gender parity, achieving the highest fairness scores. This variation suggests high bias is not an inevitable outcome but a consequence of design choices. Our work provides the most comprehensive cross-model benchmark of gender bias to date and underscores the necessity of standardized, automated evaluation tools for promoting accountability and fairness in AI development."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07050","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:32:11Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:32:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:32:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07025","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07025","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07025","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eduardo Lobo Lustoda","familyName":"Cabral","name":"Cabral, Eduardo Lobo Lustoda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulo","familyName":"Pirozelli","name":"Pirozelli, Paulo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Larissa","familyName":"Driemeier","name":"Driemeier, Larissa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"1 bit is all we need: binary normalized neural networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T13:27:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:01:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing size of large neural network models, specifically language models and foundational image models, poses deployment challenges, prompting efforts to reduce memory requirements and enhance computational efficiency. These efforts are critical to ensure practical deployment and effective utilization of these models across various applications. In this work, a novel type of neural network layers and models is developed that uses only single-bit parameters. In this novel type of models all parameters of all layers, including kernel weights and biases, only have values equal to zero or one. This novel type of models uses layers named as binary normalized layer. These binary normalized layers can be of any type, such as fully connected, convolutional, attention, etc., and they consist of slight variations of the corresponding conventional layers. To show the effectiveness of the binary normalized layers, two different models are configured to solve a multiclass image classification problem and a language decoder to predict the next token of a sequence. The model to solve the image classification has convolutional and fully connected layers, and the language model is composed of transformer blocks with multi-head attention. The results show that models with binary normalized layers present almost the same results obtained by equivalent models with real 32-bit parameters. The binary normalized layers allow to develop models that use 32 times less memory than current models and have equivalent performance. Besides, the binary normalized layers can be easily implemented on current computers using 1-bit arrays, and do not require the development of dedicated electronic hardware. This novel type of layers opens a new era for large neural network models with reduced memory requirements that can be deployed using simple and cheap hardware, such as mobile devices or only cpus."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages; 2 figures; 5 tables; 8 algorithms"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07025","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:31:36Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:31:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:31:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07022","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07022","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07022","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pavan","familyName":"Reddy","name":"Reddy, Pavan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nithin","familyName":"Reddy","name":"Reddy, Nithin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Preventing Another Tessa: Modular Safety Middleware For Health-Adjacent AI Assistants"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-07T08:43:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:01:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In 2023, the National Eating Disorders Association's (NEDA) chatbot Tessa was suspended after providing harmful weight-loss advice to vulnerable users-an avoidable failure that underscores the risks of unsafe AI in healthcare contexts. This paper examines Tessa as a case study in absent safety engineering and demonstrates how a lightweight, modular safeguard could have prevented the incident. We propose a hybrid safety middleware that combines deterministic lexical gates with an in-line large language model (LLM) policy filter, enforcing fail-closed verdicts and escalation pathways within a single model call. Using synthetic evaluations, we show that this design achieves perfect interception of unsafe prompts at baseline cost and latency, outperforming traditional multi-stage pipelines. Beyond technical remedies, we map Tessa's failure patterns to established frameworks (OWASP LLM Top10, NIST SP 800-53), connecting practical safeguards to actionable governance controls. The results highlight that robust, auditable safety in health-adjacent AI does not require heavyweight infrastructure: explicit, testable checks at the last mile are sufficient to prevent \"another Tessa\", while governance and escalation ensure sustainability in real-world deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages content, 1 page reference, 1 figure, Accepted at AAAI Fall Symposium Series"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07022","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:31:32Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:31:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:31:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07006","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.07006","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.07006","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kapil","familyName":"Madan","name":"Madan, Kapil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ArGen: Auto-Regulation of Generative AI via GRPO and Policy-as-Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; K.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-06T04:33:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:01:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces ArGen (Auto-Regulation of Generative AI systems), a framework for aligning Large Language Models (LLMs) with complex sets of configurable, machine-readable rules spanning ethical principles, operational safety protocols, and regulatory compliance standards. Moving beyond just preference-based alignment, ArGen is designed to ensure LLMs adhere to these multifaceted policies through a novel synthesis of principle-based automated reward scoring, Group Relative Policy Optimisation (GRPO), and an Open Policy Agent (OPA) inspired governance layer. This approach provides the technical foundation for achieving and demonstrating compliance with diverse and nuanced governance requirements. To showcase the framework's capability to operationalize a deeply nuanced and culturally-specific value system, we present an in-depth case study: the development of a medical AI assistant guided by principles from Dharmic ethics (such as Ahimsa and Dharma), as derived from texts like the Bhagavad Gita. This challenging application demonstrates ArGen's adaptability, achieving a 70.9% improvement in domain-scope adherence over the baseline. Through our open-source repository, we show that ArGen's methodology offers a path to 'Governable Al' systems that are technically proficient, ethically robust, and verifiably compliant for safe deployment in diverse global contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"53 pages, 7 figures, 8 tables. Open-source implementation available at: https://github.com/Principled-Evolution/argen-demo. Work explores the integration of policy-as-code for AI alignment, with a case study in culturally-nuanced, ethical AI using Dharmic principles"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.07006","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:31:06Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:31:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:31:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06982","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06982","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.06982","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomeng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaomeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhan","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Chenhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Junyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tsung-Yi","familyName":"Ho","name":"Ho, Tsung-Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CARE: Decoding Time Safety Alignment via Rollback and Introspection Intervention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-01T04:50:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:00:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in real-world applications, ensuring the safety of their outputs during decoding has become a critical challenge. However, existing decoding-time interventions, such as Contrastive Decoding, often force a severe trade-off between safety and response quality. In this work, we propose CARE, a novel framework for decoding-time safety alignment that integrates three key components: (1) a guard model for real-time safety monitoring, enabling detection of potentially unsafe content; (2) a rollback mechanism with a token buffer to correct unsafe outputs efficiently at an earlier stage without disrupting the user experience; and (3) a novel introspection-based intervention strategy, where the model generates self-reflective critiques of its previous outputs and incorporates these reflections into the context to guide subsequent decoding steps. The framework achieves a superior safety-quality trade-off by using its guard model for precise interventions, its rollback mechanism for timely corrections, and our novel introspection method for effective self-correction. Experimental results demonstrate that our framework achieves a superior balance of safety, quality, and efficiency, attaining a low harmful response rate and minimal disruption to the user experience while maintaining high response quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06982","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:30:32Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:30:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:30:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.06980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.06980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guojun","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Guojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zekun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuhuai","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Chuhuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyu","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Siyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengqi","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Chengqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"He","name":"He, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RLFactory: A Plug-and-Play Reinforcement Learning Post-Training Framework for LLM Multi-Turn Tool-Use"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-31T16:47:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-10T00:00:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models excel at basic reasoning but struggle with tasks that require interaction with external tools. We present RLFactory, a plug-and-play reinforcement learning post-training framework for multi-round tool use. RLFactory tackles (i) tool-call stability and adaptability amid tool heterogeneity and interface issues via an asyncio-based asynchronous caller and a decoupled tool/training architecture, and (ii) diverse evaluation needs via a reward layer supporting rule-based, model-judgment, and tool-verification signals. It reconstructs the MDP by introducing observation markers from tool feedback, closing the loop among model, tools, and environment, and implements a generate-parse-invoke-update workflow for dynamic policy optimization. On Search-R1 with Qwen3-4B, RLFactory achieves a 0.486 test score on the Natural Questions (NQ) dataset, surpassing larger models trained with similar techniques (e.g., Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-GRPO at 0.473), and increases training throughput by 6.8x. RLFactory provides a low-barrier, highly adaptable framework for strengthening multi-round tool use of LLMs in real-world scenarios. Code: https://github.com/Simple-Efficient/RL-Factory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.06980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-10T01:30:30Z","registered":"2025-09-10T01:30:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-10T01:30:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":47,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-09T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-10T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs%29+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+memories+OR+safety+OR+jailbreak+OR+jailbreaks+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calls%22+OR+tool+OR+tools%29%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs%29+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+agentic+OR+retrieval+OR+memory+OR+memories+OR+safety+OR+jailbreak+OR+jailbreaks+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22tool+calls%22+OR+tool+OR+tools%29%29%29"}}